# ---------- A: minerals ----------
lesson(C,S,CH,'minerals-metallic-and-non-metallic',"India's minerals: metallic and non-metallic","The mountain that became a bridge",
"The steel in a railway bridge began as red-brown rock dug out of a hill in Jharkhand or Odisha. That rock is iron ore, and it is one of the minerals that quietly build a modern country. In this lesson we learn what a resource is, what a mineral is, and where India's important minerals are found.",
["A natural resource is any matter or energy from the environment that living things use, such as air, water, soil, minerals, fossil fuels, plants and wildlife. @ natural resource",
 "Resources are renewable if they regenerate naturally after use, like solar, wind, biogas, tidal and wave energy, and non-renewable if they cannot be replaced after use, like coal, petroleum and natural gas. @ renewable / non-renewable",
 "A mineral is a natural substance of organic or inorganic origin with a definite chemical composition and physical properties, and taking it out of the earth is called mining. @ mining",
 "Mines near the surface are open pit mines, while deep mines are called shaft mines. @ open pit / shaft",
 "Four bodies look after minerals in India: the Geological Survey of India at Kolkata, the Indian Bureau of Mines at Nagpur, the Non-Ferrous Materials Technology Development Centre at Hyderabad, and the Ministry of Mines, which administers the Mines and Minerals Act of 1957. @ GSI, IBM, NFTDC",
 "On the basis of their chemical and physical properties, minerals are grouped as metallic minerals, which contain one or more metals, and non-metallic minerals, which contain no metal. @ metallic / non-metallic",
 "Iron ore is the most important mineral for making iron and steel, and it occurs as magnetite with 72.4 percent iron, hematite 69.9, goethite 62.9, limonite 55 and siderite 48.2. @ magnetite 72.4%",
 "Jharkhand leads iron ore production with about 25 percent, then Odisha with 21 percent, Karnataka with 20 percent and Chhattisgarh with 18 percent, while Andhra Pradesh and Tamil Nadu give about 5 percent each. @ Jharkhand 25%",
 "In Tamil Nadu iron ore is notable in Salem, Namakkal, Tiruvannamalai, Tiruchirappalli, Coimbatore, Madurai and Tirunelveli districts, and the Ministry of Steel plans the iron and steel industry through SAIL. @ Salem, Namakkal",
 "Manganese is a hard, brittle silvery-grey element always found with iron, and nearly 10 kg of it is needed for every tonne of steel, apart from its use in bleaching powder, insecticides, paints and batteries. @ manganese 10 kg",
 "The largest manganese deposits are in Odisha, India is the fifth largest producer in the world, and MOIL at Nagpur is the state-owned manganese mining company. @ Odisha, MOIL",
 "Copper was the first metal used by prehistoric people, it is flexible, it gives brass with zinc and bronze with tin, and it is used for wires, cables and electrical machinery, with Rajasthan holding the largest reserves, then Jharkhand and Madhya Pradesh. @ copper",
 "Bauxite is the ore of aluminium, found as surface deposits in laterite areas, and light, tough aluminium is used for aircraft and automobile engines, while bauxite also goes into cement and chemicals; the main deposits are in Odisha, and NALCO has units at Angul and Damanjodi. @ bauxite",
 "Non-metallic minerals include mica, limestone, gypsum, nitrate, potash and dolomite, and even coal and petroleum are counted here. @ non-metallic",
 "Mica, called abhrak, splits into thin clear sheets and does not conduct electricity yet withstands high voltage, so it is used in electrical goods, and it is found in Andhra Pradesh, Rajasthan, Odisha and Jharkhand. @ mica",
 "Limestone is calcium carbonate used for soda ash, caustic soda, paper, cement, iron and steel, glass and fertilisers, and gypsum, a hydrated calcium sulphate, is used for cement, fertilisers, wall board, plaster of Paris and soil conditioning. @ limestone and gypsum"],
("A steel mill melts 10 tonnes of magnetite ore. How much iron can it contain, and would siderite of the same weight give more or less?",
 [("Magnetite has 72.4 percent iron, so 10 tonnes contain 10 x 72.4 / 100 = 7.24 tonnes of iron.","Iron content is the percentage multiplied by the weight."),
  ("Siderite has only 48.2 percent iron, so 10 tonnes contain 4.82 tonnes.","Same method with the lower percentage."),
  ("So siderite gives 2.42 tonnes less iron than magnetite for the same weight.","7.24 minus 4.82 is 2.42, which is why magnetite is the richest ore.")]),
("Explain to a friend the difference between metallic and non-metallic minerals and give two examples of each with one use.",
 ["Metallic minerals contain metals, for example iron ore for steel and bauxite for aluminium","Non-metallic minerals contain no metal, for example mica for electrical goods and limestone for cement","Iron ore leading states are Jharkhand and Odisha"],
 ["Mica conducts electricity (it does not, it insulates)","Bauxite is an ore of copper (it is the ore of aluminium)"]),
["A resource is renewable if it regenerates naturally and non-renewable if it cannot be replaced.","Metallic minerals: iron, manganese, copper, bauxite. Non-metallic: mica, limestone, gypsum.","Magnetite has the most iron (72.4%); Jharkhand leads iron ore output, then Odisha.","Manganese is needed for steel (about 10 kg per tonne); bauxite gives aluminium; mica insulates; limestone and gypsum go into cement."],
["m|Which mineral is the ore of aluminium?|Bauxite|Mica~Mica is a non-metallic mineral used in electrical goods.;Gypsum~Gypsum gives plaster of Paris and cement.;Manganese~Manganese is used in making steel.|Bauxite is a hydrated aluminium oxide found in laterite areas and gives aluminium.",
 "m|Manganese is mainly used in|steel making|the paper industry~Paper uses wood pulp and bamboo, not manganese.;copper smelting~Copper smelting is a different process.;petroleum refining~Refining deals with crude oil.|About 10 kg of manganese is needed for each tonne of steel.",
 "t|Mica is a good conductor of electricity.|false|Mica does not conduct electricity but withstands high voltage, so it is used for insulation in electrical goods.",
 "m|Which form of iron ore has the highest iron content?|Magnetite|Hematite~Hematite has 69.9 percent, less than magnetite.;Limonite~Limonite has only 55 percent.;Siderite~Siderite has just 48.2 percent.|Magnetite carries 72.4 percent iron, the highest of the five forms.",
 "m|Which state is the leading producer of iron ore?|Jharkhand|Odisha~Odisha is second with about 21 percent.;Karnataka~Karnataka gives about 20 percent.;Tamil Nadu~Tamil Nadu gives only about 5 percent.|Jharkhand produces about 25 percent of India's iron ore.",
 "m|Copper alloyed with tin gives|bronze|brass~Brass is copper with zinc.;steel~Steel is iron based.;aluminium~Aluminium comes from bauxite.|Copper with tin is bronze and copper with zinc is brass.",
 "n|Nearly how many kg of manganese are needed to make one tonne of steel?|10|The textbook says nearly 10 kg of manganese is needed for one tonne of steel.",
 "s|Which state-owned manganese ore company, headquartered at Nagpur, has about half the market? Give its short name.|MOIL|MOIL, the Manganese Ore India Limited, is state-owned and based at Nagpur.",
 "m|Which pair is matched wrongly?|Gypsum - aluminium|Bauxite - aluminium~This pair is correct.;Mica - electrical goods~This pair is correct.;Limestone - cement~This pair is correct.|Gypsum is used for plaster of Paris and cement, not for aluminium, which comes from bauxite.",
 "m|Which of these is a non-metallic mineral?|Limestone|Copper~Copper is a metallic mineral.;Bauxite~Bauxite is a metallic ore of aluminium.;Manganese~Manganese is a metallic mineral.|Limestone is calcium carbonate and has no metal, so it is non-metallic."],
svg=(bars([("Magnetite",72.4),("Hematite",69.9),("Goethite",62.9),("Limonite",55),("Siderite",48.2)],"Iron content of iron ores (%)"),"Bar chart of iron content of five iron ores","Magnetite has the most iron and siderite the least."),
tname="Metallic and non-metallic minerals")
# ---------- B: energy ----------
body=DEF+title(640,"Energy resources of India")+box(240,36,160,34,"#d6eaf8",["Energy resources"])
body+=box(40,110,250,150,"#f5cba7",["Non-renewable","Coal (Gondwana, Jharkhand)","Petroleum (Mumbai High)","Natural gas (Bassein)","Thermal power (Neyveli)","Nuclear (Tarapur, Kudankulam)"])
body+=box(350,110,250,150,"#abebc6",["Renewable","Hydropower (Darjeeling 1897)","Solar (Rajasthan, Gujarat)","Wind (Muppandal, TN)","Biomass","Tidal and wave (Cambay)"])
body+=arrow(300,70,165,108)+arrow(340,70,475,108)
lesson(C,S,CH,'energy-resources-renewable-and-non-renewable',"India's energy resources","The wind farm at the land's end",
"At the southern tip of Tamil Nadu, in Kanniyakumari district, hundreds of giant wind turbines turn all day, and this is the largest concentration of wind farms at a single place in the world. Far to the north, a coal plant burns fuel that took millions of years to form. Same electricity, two very different kinds of energy.",
["The resources from which electricity is generated are called energy resources, they are the key factor for all economic activity and industrial development, and they are classified as non-renewable or renewable. @ energy resources",
 "Coal is an inflammable organic substance made mainly of hydrocarbons, found in sedimentary rocks, and mostly used to make thermal power. @ coal",
 "By carbon content coal is anthracite with 80 to 90 percent, bituminous 60 to 80, lignite 40 to 60 and peat below 40 percent. @ anthracite 80-90%",
 "Indian coal is mostly in Gondwana rocks of the peninsula, and Jharkhand, Odisha, West Bengal and Madhya Pradesh together hold nearly 90 percent of the reserves, with Jharkhand the largest producer. @ Gondwana coal",
 "About 2 percent of Indian coal is tertiary coal found in Assam and Jammu and Kashmir, and lignite or brown coal is found in Tamil Nadu, Puducherry and Kerala, mined by NLC India at Neyveli. @ lignite, Neyveli",
 "Coal India Limited at Kolkata, NLC India and Singareni Collieries are the public sector companies in coal. @ CIL, NLCIL, SCCL",
 "The word petroleum comes from the Latin petro, meaning rock, and oleum, meaning oil, so it is mineral oil, with hydrocarbons forming 90 to 95 percent of it. @ petro + oleum",
 "Petroleum powers automobiles, aeroplanes, ships and locomotives, and gives lubricants, kerosene, vaseline, tar, wax, soap and terylene as by-products. @ by-products",
 "Offshore, the Mumbai High field is the largest with about 65 percent of output, the Gujarat coast is second, and the Bassein field lies south of Mumbai High. @ Mumbai High 65%",
 "Onshore, the Brahmaputra valley in Upper Assam has the Digboi field, the oldest in the country, along with Nahorkatiya and Moran-Hugrijan. @ Digboi - oldest",
 "Natural gas usually comes with petroleum, is mainly methane, and is used for heating, cooking, electricity and as vehicle fuel in the form of CNG; GAIL of New Delhi is the largest state-owned gas company. @ natural gas, CNG",
 "Thermal power uses coal, diesel, petroleum and natural gas, NTPC was set up in 1975, and in Tamil Nadu the important thermal stations are Neyveli, Mettur, Thoothukudi and Ennore. @ thermal power",
 "Nuclear energy comes from the fission of uranium and thorium; the first station was at Tarapur near Mumbai in 1969, and others are at Rawatbhata, Kalpakkam with 440 MW, Kudankulam with 2000 MW, Narora, Kaiga and Kakrapara. @ Tarapur 1969",
 "Hydropower from running water is economic and non-polluting, the first hydro station was at Darjeeling in 1897, and NHPC is located at Faridabad. @ hydropower",
 "Solar energy turns sunlight into electricity by photovoltaic cells or by concentrated solar power, and Andhra Pradesh, Gujarat, Rajasthan, Maharashtra and Madhya Pradesh are the major producers. @ solar",
 "Wind energy is cheap and pollution free; wind farms began in India in 1986 at Okha, Ratnagiri and Thoothukudi, Tamil Nadu has the largest installation, and Muppandal-Aralvoimozhi in Kanniyakumari is the biggest wind farm concentration in the world. @ Muppandal",
 "Biomass energy comes from dung, kitchen waste, water hyacinth and crop residue, while tidal energy is best suited to the Gulf of Cambay, then the Gulf of Kutch and the Sunderbans, and a 150 kW wave power plant stands at Vizhinjam near Thiruvananthapuram. @ biomass, tidal"],
("A coal sample has 65 percent carbon and another has 45 percent. Name each type and say which gives more heat.",
 [("Check the carbon ranges: bituminous 60 to 80, lignite 40 to 60.","Coal type is decided by carbon content."),
  ("65 percent falls in the bituminous range and 45 percent in the lignite range.","Match each number to its range."),
  ("The bituminous coal has more carbon, so it is the better and hotter fuel.","More carbon means more energy per kilogram.")]),
("Explain why a country should use both renewable and non-renewable energy today, and name one example of each from Tamil Nadu.",
 ["Non-renewable resources such as coal cannot be replaced, for example lignite at Neyveli","Renewable resources regenerate, for example wind at Muppandal","Renewables are clean and cheap to run, while coal and gas give steady large power for industry"],
 ["Nuclear energy is renewable (it uses uranium and thorium, which are non-renewable)","Hydropower pollutes a lot (it is considered non-polluting)"]),
["Non-renewable: coal, petroleum, natural gas, nuclear minerals; renewable: water, sun, wind, biomass, tides.","Coal ranks anthracite, bituminous, lignite, peat by carbon; Jharkhand leads output.","Mumbai High is the largest oil field; Digboi the oldest; Tarapur the first nuclear plant (1969).","Tamil Nadu: Neyveli lignite, Kudankulam nuclear and Muppandal wind."],
["m|Which coal has the highest carbon content?|Anthracite|Lignite~Lignite has only 40 to 60 percent carbon.;Peat~Peat has less than 40 percent.;Bituminous~Bituminous has 60 to 80 percent, less than anthracite.|Anthracite has 80 to 90 percent carbon.",
 "m|Which state is the largest coal producer in India?|Jharkhand|Odisha~Odisha comes second.;Tamil Nadu~Tamil Nadu has only lignite.;Assam~Assam has a small share of tertiary coal.|Jharkhand is the largest coal producing state.",
 "t|The word petroleum comes from Latin words meaning rock and oil.|true|Petro means rock and oleum means oil, so petroleum is oil from rocks.",
 "m|Which is India's largest offshore oil field?|Mumbai High|Digboi~Digboi is the oldest onshore field in Assam.;Bassein~Bassein lies south of Mumbai High and is smaller.;Nahorkatiya~Nahorkatiya is an onshore field in Assam.|Mumbai High gives about 65 percent of the oil output.",
 "m|Where was India's first nuclear power station set up?|Tarapur|Kalpakkam~Kalpakkam in Tamil Nadu came later.;Kudankulam~Kudankulam is the 2000 MW plant in Tamil Nadu.;Narora~Narora is in Uttar Pradesh.|Tarapur near Mumbai started in 1969.",
 "m|The Muppandal wind farm is in which district?|Kanniyakumari|Thoothukudi~Thoothukudi had an early wind farm but Muppandal is not there.;Coimbatore~Coimbatore is inland.;Tirunelveli~Muppandal-Aralvoimozhi lies in Kanniyakumari.|Muppandal-Aralvoimozhi in Kanniyakumari is the largest wind farm concentration in the world.",
 "n|What is the capacity in MW of the Kudankulam nuclear station given in the textbook?|2000|The textbook lists Kudankulam in Tamil Nadu as 2,000 MW.",
 "s|Which gas is the chief component of natural gas?|methane|Natural gas is mainly methane with small amounts of other hydrocarbons.",
 "m|Lignite or brown coal is mainly found in|Tamil Nadu, Puducherry and Kerala|Jharkhand and Odisha~These hold the Gondwana coal.;Assam and Jammu and Kashmir~These have a small amount of tertiary coal.;Punjab and Haryana~They have no major coal.|Neyveli in Tamil Nadu is the best known lignite area.",
 "m|Which of these is a renewable energy source?|Tidal energy|Coal~Coal cannot be replaced once used.;Natural gas~Natural gas is a fossil fuel.;Uranium~Nuclear minerals are non-renewable.|Tides come again and again, so tidal energy is renewable."],
svg=(svg(640,290,body),"Tree diagram splitting energy resources into non-renewable and renewable","Non-renewable and renewable energy with Indian examples."),
tname="Energy resources: renewable and non-renewable")
# ---------- C: industries ----------
body=DEF+title(640,"Industries by raw material")+box(250,36,140,34,"#d6eaf8",["Industries"])
body+=box(20,110,190,150,"#f9e79f",["Agro-based","Cotton textile","Jute","Silk","Sugar"])
body+=box(225,110,190,150,"#abebc6",["Forest-based","Paper","Lac","Sports goods","Plywood"])
body+=box(430,110,190,150,"#f5cba7",["Mineral-based","Iron and steel","Automobile","Electrical","Software (service)"])
