lesson(C,S,CH,'industries-agro-forest-and-mineral-based',"India's industries and their challenges","Why does the sugar mill stand in the cane field?",
"No one builds a sugar mill in a big city; they stand next to sugarcane fields. Cane loses weight and sugar as it dries, and it is bulky to carry. Where an industry stands is never an accident; it follows its raw material. This lesson takes the book's industries one by one and asks that same question each time.",
["An industry converts raw materials into finished goods, which is why this sector is called the value addition sector, and by raw material industries are agro-based, forest-based or mineral-based. @ value addition",
 "Cotton textiles is the broad term covering cotton, jute, wool, silk and synthetic fibre; India's textile sector is the second largest in the world and the third largest producer of cotton. @ cotton textile",
 "The first cotton mill was set up at Fort Gloster near Kolkata in 1818, and the cotton textile industry is today the largest organised modern industry of India. @ Fort Gloster 1818",
 "Mumbai is called the Manchester of India because black cotton soil, a humid climate, the port, hydropower, a good market and transport all help the mills there. @ Mumbai",
 "Coimbatore, with about 200 of Tamil Nadu's 435 mills, is the Manchester of South India, and Erode, Tiruppur, Karur, Madurai, Salem and Virudhunagar are other centres. @ Coimbatore",
 "Jute is the golden fibre, a natural, renewable, biodegradable material; the first jute mill started at Rishra near Kolkata in 1854, and mills crowd the Hooghly river belt in West Bengal. @ jute",
 "India is the top producer of raw jute and makes gunny bags, canvas, hessian, twine and carpets from it. @ gunny bags",
 "India is the second largest producer of raw silk after China, Karnataka is the largest silk producing state, and the Central Silk Technological Research Institute at Bengaluru was set up in 1983. @ silk",
 "Sugar is made from sugarcane, India is the second largest producer after Brazil, and mills are located near the cane fields because cane is bulky and loses weight; Uttar Pradesh is the leading producer, followed by Maharashtra and Karnataka. @ sugar",
 "Paper is the main forest-based industry using wood pulp, bamboo, salai and sabai grass, waste paper and bagasse; the first mill was at Serampore in 1812, and West Bengal is the largest producer. @ paper",
 "Iron and steel is called a basic metallurgical industry because machines, locomotives, automobiles and railways use it as raw material, so a country's steel capacity shows its industrial development. @ iron and steel",
 "The first attempt to make iron and steel was at Porto Novo in Tamil Nadu in 1830, and modern industry began with Tata Iron and Steel Company at Sakchi, now Jamshedpur, in 1907. @ Jamshedpur 1907",
 "Steel is concentrated in Jharkhand, West Bengal and Odisha because of the nearness of Jharia, Raniganj, Bokaro and Karanpura coalfields and the iron ore of Mayurbhanj and Keonjhar, with limestone, dolomite and manganese close by. @ coal + iron ore",
 "Public sector steel plants include Bhilai with Russia in 1957, Durgapur with Britain in 1959, Rourkela with Germany in 1965, Bokaro with Russia in 1972, and Salem Steel in Tamil Nadu in 1982 for stainless steel. @ Bhilai, Durgapur, Rourkela",
 "India's first automobile company was Premier Automobiles at Kurla in Mumbai in 1947, Chennai is the Detroit of Asia, and India is the seventh largest producer of automobiles. @ Chennai - Detroit of Asia",
 "Heavy electrical goods come from BHEL with plants at Haridwar, Bhopal, Hyderabad, Jhansi, Bengaluru and Tiruchirappalli, and Bengaluru is the Electronic Capital of India. @ BHEL, Bengaluru",
 "The software industry began in 1970 with Tata Consultancy Services, now has over 500 companies exporting to about 95 countries, and the Make in India programme of 2014 supports manufacturing. @ software 1970",
 "Indian industries face power shortage, lack of large land blocks, poor access to credit, high interest rates, scarce cheap labour, lack of technical training and poor living conditions near industrial estates. @ challenges"],
("Why is a sugar mill built near sugarcane fields while an electronics factory can stand in a city like Bengaluru?",
 [("Sugarcane is bulky and loses weight and sugar as time passes after harvest.","This is the textbook's reason."),
  ("So transport cost and loss are lowest if the mill is next to the fields.","Raw material that is heavy and perishable pulls the industry toward it."),
  ("Electronic parts are small, light and durable, so they can be brought from anywhere, and the factory can stand where skilled people and markets are.","Location follows the nature of the raw material.")]),
("Explain how raw material decides where an industry stands, using steel in Jamshedpur and cotton mills in Mumbai.",
 ["Steel needs coal and iron ore, found in Jharkhand, Odisha and West Bengal","Cotton mills at Mumbai got black cotton soil, port, humid climate and market","Heavy or perishable raw materials pull the industry toward them"],
 ["The first cotton mill was at Mumbai (it was at Fort Gloster near Kolkata)","Sugar mills are built far from cane fields (they stand near them)"]),
["Industries are agro-based, forest-based or mineral-based according to the raw material.","Cotton: Mumbai is the Manchester of India, Coimbatore the Manchester of South India; jute is the golden fibre.","Iron and steel started at Jamshedpur in 1907 and clusters near coal and iron ore.","Chennai is the Detroit of Asia, Bengaluru the Electronic Capital; challenges include power, land, credit and training."],
["m|Which city is called the Manchester of India?|Mumbai|Coimbatore~Coimbatore is the Manchester of South India.;Kolkata~Kolkata is the jute centre.;Erode~Erode is another textile town in Tamil Nadu.|Mumbai has many cotton mills helped by black soil, port and market.",
 "m|Where was the first cotton textile mill of India set up?|Fort Gloster near Kolkata|Mumbai~Mumbai grew later as the great cotton centre.;Coimbatore~Coimbatore is the Manchester of South India.;Ahmedabad~The textbook names Fort Gloster.|The first mill came up in 1818 at Fort Gloster near Kolkata.",
 "t|Jute is called the golden fibre.|true|Jute meets packing needs with natural, renewable, biodegradable products and is called the golden fibre.",
 "m|Sugar mills are located near sugarcane fields because cane is|bulky and loses weight|a rare crop~Cane is widely grown.;very light~It is heavy and bulky.;used only in winter~That is not the reason given.|Cane is weight-losing and bulky to transport.",
 "m|Which city is nicknamed the Detroit of Asia?|Chennai|Pune~Pune is an automobile centre but not the Detroit of Asia.;Jamshedpur~Jamshedpur is the steel city.;Bengaluru~Bengaluru is the Electronic Capital of India.|Chennai has many automobile and allied units.",
 "m|Which city is called the Electronic Capital of India?|Bengaluru|Hyderabad~Hyderabad is another centre of electronic goods.;Chennai~Chennai is the Detroit of Asia.;Kolkata~Kolkata is a jute centre.|Bengaluru is the largest producer of electronic goods.",
 "n|In which year was the Tata Iron and Steel Company set up at Sakchi?|1907|Modernisation of iron and steel began in 1907 at Sakchi, now Jamshedpur.",
 "s|Which company, with a plant at Tiruchirappalli, makes turbines and boilers? Give its short name.|BHEL/Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited/Bharat Heavy Electricals Ltd|Bharat Heavy Electricals Limited has plants at Haridwar, Bhopal, Hyderabad, Jhansi, Bengaluru and Tiruchirappalli.",
 "m|Which is NOT given as a challenge of Indian industries?|Too many well-trained workers|Poor access to credit~This is listed as a challenge.;Shortage of power supply~This is listed as a challenge.;Non-availability of large blocks of land~This is listed as a challenge.|The book lists lack of technical and vocational training, not an excess of trained workers.",
 "m|Iron and steel is concentrated in Jharkhand, West Bengal and Odisha because of nearness to|coalfields and iron ore mines|cotton fields~Cotton is not a steel raw material.;sugarcane fields~Sugarcane is for sugar mills.;wind farms~Steel does not depend on wind farms.|Jharia, Raniganj, Bokaro coal and Mayurbhanj, Keonjhar iron ore lie close."],
svg=(svg(640,290,body),"Tree diagram splitting industries into agro-based, forest-based and mineral-based","Industries grouped by their raw material."),
tname="Industries: agro, forest and mineral based")
